## What happened

In **.NET 11**, Apple's removal of DSA support from macOS is now surfaced by the runtime. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) **throws on macOS**. Windows and Linux are unaffected — DSA still works there.

## How to fix it

Since you're only **verifying** legacy signatures, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Replace DSA with a modern algorithm (recommended)

If you control both the signing and verification sides, migrate to **ECDSA** or **RSA**:

```csharp
// Before (.NET 10)
using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(publicKeyParams);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);

// After (.NET 11)
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportParameters(publicKeyParams);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 2 — Platform-guard DSA for legacy verification only

If you **must** verify existing DSA signatures and can't re-sign, guard the code so it only runs on platforms that still support DSA:

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
        "DSA signature verification is not supported on macOS with .NET 11+.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
// ... verify legacy signature on Windows/Linux
```

**Bottom line:** DSA is a legacy algorithm. The best long-term fix is migrating to ECDSA or RSA for all platforms.
